#!/usr/bin/env python3
# mise description="Quality frontier: graded ladders per task class, run per model and harness variant, summarised as a frontier. Usage: mise run bench-frontier -- gen|validate|run|summary|cloud-probe|selftest (--help on each)"
"""How far each task class can be pushed before quality breaks, and whether the
limit is the model or the harness. Design: reports/2026-09-25-quality-frontier/design.md.

  gen       generate the ladders from a checkout of the pinned target (no GPU)
  validate  build-check every generated task: the reference solution must build,
            the known-bad ones must not, every debug break must fail the suite
            (CPU only, ~40 min; writes bench/frontier/validated-<sha>.json)
  run       run ladders for one local profile (--profile) or a cloud model (--cloud)
            under one harness variant; writes bench/frontier-<key>-<variant>-<stamp>.json
  summary   per-rung pass rates, Wilson bounds, verdicts, frontiers and a logistic fit
  cloud-probe  one real cloud session through the run's workspace layout; passes only
            if the model's file write lands in that workspace (~$0.05)
  selftest  generator, verifiers, runner and summary against a fixture repo and fake
            models (no GPU, no network, no servers)

The runner is bench-cheap-ops' semantics, not a copy of them: the helpers that
make a task measurable (clean workspace, pinned clone, hidden git history, reset,
break, server restart, served-model check, loop count) are lifted out of
bench-cheap-ops at start-up and run unchanged, so a fix there reaches this suite.
bench-cheap-ops itself is not modified and still reads only bench/tasks.json.
"""
